Close the -ENOTDIR existence oracle at every choke point.

An -ENOTDIR proves a name EXISTS -- the walk stepped into something that is not
a directory. An absent name gives -ENOENT. Every path-resolving syscall that can
be made to want a directory is therefore a one-syscall existence probe of a
hidden path:

  stat(p "/zzz")                    chdir(p)
  stat(p "/")                       open(p, O_PATH|O_DIRECTORY)
  open(p, O_DIRECTORY)              inotify_add_watch(p, IN_ONLYDIR)
  mkdirat(p "/d")                   mknodat / symlinkat / linkat target
  unlinkat(p "/d")                  renameat(..., p "/d")

THREE SITES, AND EACH ONE WAS FOUND THE HARD WAY

  path_lookupat()   everything that is not an open(2) and not a create
  path_parentat()   the CREATE family -- filename_create() and friends
  do_open()         open(p, O_DIRECTORY), which routes through neither

None of the three is reachable from the others.

*** HOW THE FIRST HUNK USED TO LAND IN THE SECOND FUNCTION ***

This is why the hunks carry FOUR lines of context and why the first line of each
is the one assignment that differs. It is not style.

path_lookupat() and path_parentat() end with the SAME five lines:

	if (!err) {
		<assign>
		nd->path.mnt = NULL;
		nd->path.dentry = NULL;
	}
	terminate_walk(nd);
	return err;
}

An earlier revision used three lines of leading context, starting at
`nd->path.mnt = NULL;`. That pre-image occurs TWICE in fs/namei.c on every one of
the five fleet trees, and `patch` resolves an ambiguous pre-image by proximity to
the @@ line number -- which is pinned to 6.12. Measured, with `patch -p1 -F0` and
no fuzz reported anywhere:

	5.10   guard landed in path_parentat()   <- not where it was aimed
	5.15   guard landed in path_parentat()   <- not where it was aimed
	6.1    guard landed in path_parentat()   <- not where it was aimed
	6.6    guard landed in path_parentat()   <- not where it was aimed
	6.12   guard landed in path_lookupat()      as intended

So on four of five kernels stat(p "/zzz"), stat(p "/"), chdir(p),
open(O_PATH|O_DIRECTORY) and inotify IN_ONLYDIR were all still open. Nothing
reported it: the patch applied at fuzz 0, the build compiled, and every existing
assertion ("fs/namei.c contains ghost_hidden_path") passed.

AND THE ACCIDENT WAS HALF RIGHT. Pinning the guard to path_lookupat() alone --
the obvious fix -- left the CREATE family open on all five, because
filename_create() reaches -ENOTDIR through filename_parentat() -> path_parentat()
and never gets far enough for ghost_create.patch's dentry guard to see anything.
That was MEASURED on a live OP15 (Android 16, 6.12.23) with a compiled probe, as
blocked uid 10384, against a hidden path and an absent sibling in the same
directory. Twenty-one of twenty-two probes matched exactly; one did not:

	mkdirat(p "/d")   hidden -> ENOTDIR   absent -> ENOENT

One syscall, no privilege, no control path, still open after every other guard in
this directory had closed. So the answer is BOTH functions, not one instead of
the other, and `*path = nd->path;` / `*parent = nd->path;` are what make the two
pre-images unique -- 2 occurrences without them, 1 with, counted on every tree.

THE LESSON, FOR THE NEXT PATCH IN THIS DIRECTORY: fuzz 0 is necessary and NOT
sufficient. A hunk whose pre-image appears more than once lands wherever the line
numbers say, and the line numbers are pinned to one tree. Count your pre-image
before you trust the dry-run -- `scripts/check_preimages.py` does it for you, and
CI runs it. And when a guard turns out to have been in the "wrong" place, check
what it was covering there before removing it.

WHAT THIS REPLACES

The two files this supersedes -- ghost_notdir.patch and ghost_notdir_5_10.patch
-- guarded ONE site: link_path_walk()'s non-final-component `d_can_lookup()`
bail. That closed `stat(p "/zzz")` and nothing else, and it put a guard inside
link_path_walk(), the hottest function in the VFS and the one hunk in this patch
set that carried a documented bootloop risk. link_path_walk() is no longer
touched by _ghost at all.

It also needed two variants, because 5.10 spells the unlazy step unlazy_walk()
where 5.15+ spells it try_to_unlazy(). The choke points below do not care which,
so ONE file covers 5.10 / 5.15 / 6.1 / 6.6 / 6.12.

do_open() AND ghost_open.patch

ghost_open.patch places a fourth guard in do_open(), about fifteen lines above
this file's third hunk, on write-intent and O_CREAT opens. The two do not overlap
textually and apply in either order. With both applied the earlier guard answers
open(p, O_DIRECTORY|O_WRONLY) first; this hunk stays as the narrower,
independently verified backstop for the read-only forms.

THE RCU TEST IS THE DESIGN WORK

ghost_hidden_path() renders d_path(), which needs a reference-counted path, and
-ENOTDIR can reach the end of either walker from sites that never unlazied:
path_init() rejects a non-directory dfd or a non-directory preset root with
`nd->flags` already carrying LOOKUP_RCU, before rcu_read_lock() and before
nd->path is assigned. Both guards therefore test `!(nd->flags & LOOKUP_RCU)` AND
`nd->path.dentry` before touching the predicate.

Nothing is lost by that: link_path_walk()'s site calls
try_to_unlazy()/unlazy_walk() and returns -ECHILD if it cannot, and
complete_walk() runs ahead of the LOOKUP_DIRECTORY test. Every probe listed at
the top reaches the predicate in ref-walk mode. do_open() needs no such test --
complete_walk() ran inside open_last_lookups() before it.

APPLIES TO

One file, three hunks, 5.10 through 6.12. Every pre-image was counted in every
tree before this was generated: path_lookupat()'s and path_parentat()'s are
unique once their `*path` / `*parent` line is included, and do_open()'s
`if ((nd->flags & LOOKUP_DIRECTORY) && ...)` is unique on its own
(path_lookupat() spells its test `if (!err && nd->flags & ...)`). The two walker
hunks sit 38-43 lines apart on every tree, so -U4 keeps them separate.

Verified with `patch -p1 -F0 --dry-run` on the pinned OnePlus trees for 5.10,
5.15, 6.1, 6.6 and 6.12, then APPLIED for real on each with the landing function
asserted by name -- which `scripts/apply_nomount_stack.sh verify ghost` now does
too, for path_lookupat() AND path_parentat(), so neither can go missing again.
fs/namei.o compiles clean on all five (ARCH=arm64 LLVM=1). Generated by editing
the real file and running `diff -u4 -p`, never by hand.

NOT covered: 4.9 / 4.14 / 4.19 / 5.4, which are not in the builder fleet and
have no tree here to verify against.
